<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuareg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tuareg are a Berber-speaking, traditionally nomadic people of the central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>. They controlled important desert routes and oases and at times raided or dominated the cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger bend</a>. They seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> in 1433, and their pressure features in the histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> and the Kanem-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuareg.jpg" alt="Tuareg" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amine loua · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homme_toureg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Resistance continued long after the formal conquest: the Bani–Volta war of 1915–1916 in the west of present-day Burkina Faso and Mali, and the <b>Tuareg</b> revolts of 1916–1917 led by Kaocen in Niger and Firhoun in present-day Mali, were among the largest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Alooma's campaigns against the Bulala, <b>Tuareg</b> groups and Hausa states such as Kano, recorded by his chronicler Ahmad ibn Furtu, restored and expanded the empire's influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population included Mandinka, Soninke, Fulani, Songhai and <b>Tuareg</b> communities, which encouraged cultural exchange and bound the outlying regions into one imperial economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His first great success was the Songhai conquest of Timbuktu in 1468, taken from the <b>Tuareg</b> who had held it since Mali's decline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Punic words survive in modern Berber dialects, such as agadir, ‘wall’, from Punic gader, and Tifinagh, the name of the Libyco-Berber alphabet still used irregularly by the <b>Tuareg</b>, may derive from a cognate of the name ‘Punic’, though a direct descent of the script is debated.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Western writers often compare tizita to the blues; other comparisons are the Portuguese saudade, the assouf of the <b>Tuareg</b>, the Romanian dor and the German Sehnsucht.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tizita">Tizita</a></li></ul></section>
